<Project>

  <!--
    Cove.Sdk + Cove.Core wiring, centralized here so individual extension .csproj files under
    extensions/<Name>/src/<Name>/ don't each restate the local-vs-package selection or the
    relative-path math back to the sibling ../cove checkout. Cove.Sdk transitively carries
    Cove.Plugins and Cove.Core (Cove.Sdk -> Cove.Plugins -> Cove.Core), so referencing Cove.Sdk
    alone is sufficient — extension projects should NOT add a direct Cove.Plugins reference.

    Cove.Data is intentionally NOT wired here: it sits outside the Cove.Sdk/Cove.Plugins/Cove.Core
    chain and is consumed test-only (e.g. Renamer.Tests' CoveContextFactory). Each test project keeps
    its own explicit Exists()-guarded ProjectReference for that.

    Scoped to non-test projects only (MSBuildProjectName not ending in ".Tests"): a *.Tests project
    already receives Cove.Sdk/Cove.Plugins/Cove.Core transitively through its ProjectReference to the
    extension project this wiring targets. Applying this ItemGroup unconditionally to *.Tests projects
    too would add Cove.Sdk as a SECOND, directly-referenced Private=false dependency on the same test
    project — which (unlike the single-hop transitive reference) MSBuild resolves as non-copy-local,
    so Cove.Sdk.dll silently drops out of the test project's own bin/ output and xUnit fails at
    reflection time with "Could not load file or assembly 'Cove.Sdk'". Excluding *.Tests here keeps
    each test project's only path to Cove.Sdk the transitive one through its extension ProjectReference,
    matching the single-extension-repo template's original per-project (not root-centralized) wiring.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'source' AND !$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(CoveSdkProjectPath)" Condition="Exists('$(CoveSdkProjectPath)')">
      <Private>false</Private>
      <ExcludeAssets>runtime</ExcludeAssets>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Both non-source modes take the SAME NuGet Cove.Sdk, and `assemblies` mode deliberately does not
    swap it for an image reference: a raw assembly reference carries no NuGet dependency graph, so
    Cove.Plugins' Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational stops being supplied and the shipping
    project loses DbContext. Measured, as is the narrower variant — cutting the package's flow to
    consumers with PrivateAssets, so a test project sees only its own image references — which fails
    the compile instead with CS1705: the image's Cove.Data binds a different EF Core patch than this
    repo pins, and the NuGet path is what was reconciling the two.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' != 'source' AND !$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <PackageReference Include="Cove.Sdk" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Local-source build only: the Cove.Sdk host-assembly stripping rules ship in the NuGet package's
    buildTransitive/ folder, so they are auto-imported on the PackageReference (publish) path but NOT
    when Cove.Sdk is consumed as a local ProjectReference. Import them explicitly so the local-source
    publish set is stripped identically to the NuGet path — otherwise the transitive Cove.Core.dll
    (and friends) leaks into the published output. Excluded for *.Tests projects (see above): a test
    project is never published/deployed, so it must keep its full runtime closure and never import
    the host-provided-assembly strip target at all.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(CoveSdkTargetsPath)"
          Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'source' AND !$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests')) AND Exists('$(CoveSdkTargetsPath)')" />

  <!--
    The gate on the Cove location. Directory.Build.props can only COMPUTE the mode; stopping a build
    needs a target, and this one runs BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild" so it lands ahead of the
    compiler and a wrong location reports as one plain error rather than as a wall of CS0234.

    Printing the resolved ABSOLUTE path is a requirement of the design, not a courtesy: it is the only
    thing that made Git Bash's MSYS rewriting of a POSIX COVE_REPO into a drive-lettered path visible
    at all, and that class of silent path mangling has already disabled gates in this repo.

    Absence stays a legitimate state — `none` is the clean-clone default and builds happily. What is
    refused is a mode that cannot deliver what it names: falling back from one of these would compile
    a smaller test set and still report success, which is the whole defect this seam exists to remove.
  -->
  <!--
    The companion gate to AssertCoveLocation, and the one that catches what no warning does. In
    `assemblies` mode a test project resolves Cove twice — once from the image references it declares,
    once from the NuGet packages that reach it through the extension project's graph — and both are
    copied to the same output file names. The image copy currently wins, but nothing SAYS so — copy
    order decides it, not any declared rule. When the NuGet copy won instead, most of the suite failed
    at run time with TypeLoadException on entity types the older Cove.Core does not carry, against a
    build that reported zero errors and zero warnings. Measured, not theorised, which is why this
    target exists at all. Reading the versions back out of the output
    turns "right by copy order" into "checked", and the failure is then one line naming both versions
    instead of a wall of unrelated-looking test failures.
  -->
  <Target Name="AssertCoveOutputClosure"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'assemblies' AND $(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_CoveOutputAssembly Include="$(TargetDir)Cove.Core.dll;$(TargetDir)Cove.Data.dll;$(TargetDir)Cove.Plugins.dll;$(TargetDir)Cove.Sdk.dll" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="!Exists('%(_CoveOutputAssembly.Identity)')"
           Text="Cove assemblies mode was selected but '%(_CoveOutputAssembly.Identity)' is not in the test output, so the suite would fail to load rather than fail an assertion." />

    <GetAssemblyIdentity AssemblyFiles="@(_CoveOutputAssembly)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Assemblies" ItemName="_CoveOutputIdentity" />
    </GetAssemblyIdentity>

    <Error Condition="'%(_CoveOutputIdentity.Version)' != '$(CoveTestAssemblyVersion)'"
           Text="'%(_CoveOutputIdentity.Identity)' in the test output is version %(_CoveOutputIdentity.Version), but assemblies mode expects the image's $(CoveTestAssemblyVersion). The NuGet copy of a Cove assembly has displaced the image one, which produces type-load failures at run time and no warning at build time. Re-run 'node scripts/fetch-cove-assemblies.mjs', or bump CoveTestAssemblyVersion if the image itself moved — never CoveMinVersion, which means something else." />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="AssertCoveLocation" BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild">
    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Cove: mode=$(CoveSourceMode) repoRoot='$(CoveRepoRootResolved)' assembliesDir='$(CoveAssembliesDirResolved)'" />

    <Error Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' != 'source' AND '$(CoveSourceMode)' != 'assemblies' AND '$(CoveSourceMode)' != 'none'"
           Text="CoveSourceMode is '$(CoveSourceMode)', which is not one of source, assemblies or none. Set -p:CoveSourceMode (or COVE_SOURCE_MODE) to one of those three; an unrecognised mode wires up neither references nor exclusions and would fail far from its cause." />

    <Error Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'source' AND !Exists('$(CoveDataProjectPath)')"
           Text="Cove source mode was selected but Cove.Data.csproj is not at '$(CoveDataProjectPath)' (resolved CoveRepoRoot='$(CoveRepoRootResolved)'). Point COVE_REPO or -p:CoveRepoRoot at a Cove checkout, or select another mode. Refusing to fall back — a silent fallback would compile a smaller test set and still report success." />

    <Error Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'assemblies' AND !Exists('$(CoveDataAssemblyPath)')"
           Text="Cove assemblies mode was selected but Cove.Data.dll is not at '$(CoveDataAssemblyPath)' (resolved CoveAssembliesDir='$(CoveAssembliesDirResolved)'). Run 'node scripts/fetch-cove-assemblies.mjs --out &lt;dir&gt;' and point COVE_ASSEMBLIES or -p:CoveAssembliesDir at it. Refusing to fall back — a silent fallback would compile a smaller test set and still report success." />

    <!-- The belt on the precedence above: today no arm can reach `none` while a location was supplied
         explicitly, so this cannot fire. It is here so that a future edit which reintroduces the
         fall-through is a build error rather than a quietly smaller run. A caller who pinned the mode
         (or the older UseLocalCoveSource spelling) said `none` on purpose and is left alone. -->
    <Error Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'none' AND '$(_CoveLocationWasExplicit)' == 'true' AND '$(_CoveModeWasPinned)' != 'true'"
           Text="A Cove location was supplied explicitly (resolved CoveRepoRoot='$(CoveRepoRootResolved)', CoveAssembliesDir='$(CoveAssembliesDirResolved)') but the resolved mode is 'none'. Refusing to fall back to the cove-absent branch — it would compile a smaller test set and still report success." />

    <!--
      The stale-extraction half. A path that resolves can still hold last month's assemblies, and the
      only symptom would be type-load failures far from the cause. GetAssemblyIdentity is MSBuild's
      own task, so this costs no tooling.

      Assert the ASSEMBLY version — $(CoveTestAssemblyVersion) — never the informational one, whose
      `-dev.<build>` suffix moves on every nightly rebuild while the assembly version does not. An
      equality guard on the informational string would go red daily for no reason and would be
      switched off, which is how a gate dies.

      This guard is EXPECTED to fail when upstream bumps the minor, and that failure is the guard
      working. The remedy is a one-line CoveTestAssemblyVersion bump after checking the change is
      wanted. CoveMinVersion is a DIFFERENT property with a different meaning — the floor the extension
      advertises to users — and editing it to make this pass would silently re-advertise that floor.
    -->
    <GetAssemblyIdentity Condition="'$(CoveSourceMode)' == 'assemblies' AND Exists('$(CoveDataAssemblyPath)')"
                         AssemblyFiles="$(CoveDataAssemblyPath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Assemblies" ItemName="_CoveDataIdentity" />
    </GetAssemblyIdentity>

    <Error Condition="'@(_CoveDataIdentity)' != '' AND '%(_CoveDataIdentity.Version)' != '$(CoveTestAssemblyVersion)'"
           Text="Cove.Data.dll at '$(CoveDataAssemblyPath)' is version %(_CoveDataIdentity.Version), but this repo tests against $(CoveTestAssemblyVersion). Re-run 'node scripts/fetch-cove-assemblies.mjs' to re-extract, or — if upstream really did move — bump CoveTestAssemblyVersion. Do NOT edit CoveMinVersion to satisfy this: that property is the floor the extension advertises, not the version CI tests against." />
  </Target>

</Project>
